WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.300
Plasma pH follows one ratio.

2
00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:06.440
The lungs keep its bottom; the kidney keeps its top.

3
00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:11.160
When a disturbance pushes one term, what does the other keeper do?

4
00:00:14.617 --> 00:00:18.657
If carbon dioxide moves first, the disturbance is respiratory.

5
00:00:20.017 --> 00:00:22.537
If bicarbonate moves first, it is metabolic.

6
00:00:23.957 --> 00:00:28.377
Take a metabolic acidosis: bicarbonate falls from 24 to 12.

7
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:34.220
Dissolved CO2 is 0.03 times its pressure.

8
00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:38.740
At 40 mmHg, that is 1.2.

9
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.700
Hold it there, and the ratio drops to 10 to 1.

10
00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:46.000
pH would fall to 7.1.

11
00:00:46.983 --> 00:00:52.083
But within minutes, breathing begins to deepen, and carbon dioxide falls too.

12
00:00:53.363 --> 00:00:57.023
It reaches its full extent over about 12 to 24 hours.

13
00:00:58.343 --> 00:01:01.363
Both terms fall, so the ratio climbs back toward 20.

14
00:01:02.517 --> 00:01:08.197
In this made-up patient, CO2 settles at 27 mmHg.

15
00:01:09.457 --> 00:01:11.497
That leaves 0.81 dissolved.

16
00:01:12.857 --> 00:01:16.977
The ratio is 14.8, and pH is 7.27.

17
00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:20.607
Full repair would mean 20 to 1 again.

18
00:01:21.907 --> 00:01:28.007
With bicarbonate at 12, how low would CO2 need to fall, in mmHg?

19
00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:38.233
To 20 mmHg. The lungs stopped at 27.

20
00:01:39.553 --> 00:01:44.413
They bought back about 0.17 of a pH unit, but not all of it.

21
00:01:45.417 --> 00:01:46.937
Here is the one idea.

22
00:01:48.577 --> 00:01:52.277
Compensation moves the other term in the same direction as the first.

23
00:01:53.397 --> 00:01:56.657
The ratio heads back toward 20, usually partway.

24
00:01:57.937 --> 00:02:02.397
Sometimes, in chronic respiratory disorders, it gets nearly all the way.

25
00:02:03.597 --> 00:02:06.257
It never carries pH past 7.4.

26
00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:14.080
For metabolic acidosis, Winter's formula expects 1.5 times bicarbonate, plus 8, give

27
00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:19.560
or take 2. Here, that is 24 to 28, so 27 fits.

28
00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:24.080
It is a line fitted to people, with scatter, not a law.

29
00:02:25.133 --> 00:02:29.733
When CO2 rises first, the kidney answers the same way.

30
00:02:30.973 --> 00:02:37.453
At once, buffering raises bicarbonate about 1 mmol/L for every 10 mmHg.

31
00:02:38.793 --> 00:02:43.773
Over days, the kidney makes new bicarbonate, about 5 per 10, in one study.

32
00:02:45.113 --> 00:02:46.453
Full repair would need 6.

33
00:02:47.517 --> 00:02:49.737
So read a blood gas against this rule.

34
00:02:51.417 --> 00:02:56.457
Numbers moving in opposite directions, or a compensation far off the fit, mean two

35
00:02:56.457 --> 00:02:57.817
things are going on at once.

36
00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:04.110
So the lungs answer a metabolic push, and the kidney answers a respiratory one.

37
00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:09.250
Each pulls pH back toward 7.4, and neither overshoots.
